INT. COUNTY PAROLE OFFICE - WAITING AREA - DAY

Fluorescent hum. A ticket machine. Plastic chairs bolted to the floor. Somewhere a door buzzes and does not open.

Darnell Whitfield, 40s, a gray jacket two sizes too big, sits with his hands flat on his knees. Eleven years inside. He studies the floor tiles like they might shift.

Officer Imelda Cruz, 30s, parole officer, appears at the inner door with a folder. Tired eyes. Kind ones.

CRUZ
Mr. Whitfield. Come on back.

He rises. His knees pop. He follows.

INT. COUNTY PAROLE OFFICE - CRUZ'S OFFICE - CONTINUOUS

A cramped room. A dying ficus. A window onto a brick wall. Cruz sits. Darnell does not, until she nods.

Behind the glass partition, a second desk. Gideon Pratt, 50s, supervisor, pretends to type. He is not typing.

CRUZ
How's the halfway house treating you, Darnell?

DARNELL
Quiet. Too quiet, some nights.

CRUZ
That's normal. Sleep comes.

A sound in the ceiling. A tick. A pause. Another tick. Darnell's eyes go up. Cruz's don't.

DARNELL
You hear that?

CRUZ
Old building. Pipes.

The ticking again. Slower. Closer to the vent.

GIDEON
(through the glass, flat)
Whitfield. Employment verification.

Darnell hands over a crumpled slip. Gideon reads it without looking up.

GIDEON (CONT'D)
Dock work. Nights. Forty hours.

DARNELL
Yes sir.

GIDEON
Don't "sir" me. Save it for the judge.

Cruz shoots Gideon a look through the glass. He shrugs and goes back to not typing.

The door opens. Marisol Teague, 20s, intern, leans in with a stack of forms.

MARISOL
Sorry. Officer Cruz? The drug screen, it's in. He's clean.

CRUZ
Thank you, Mari.

Marisol lingers, eyeing Darnell with open curiosity. Cruz clears her throat. Marisol backs out.

The tick in the ceiling stops. The silence that replaces it is worse.

DARNELL
(quiet)
Ms. Cruz. Eleven years, I counted ceiling tiles. Four hundred and twelve. Same as this room.

CRUZ
(gently)
You don't have to count anymore.

DARNELL
Habit.

This is the last time he will sit in a room where someone else holds the door.

CRUZ
Next appointment's the fourteenth. Same time. Don't be late, Darnell.

DARNELL
Wouldn't dream of it.

He stands. Shakes her hand. His grip lingers a second too long.

DARNELL (CONT'D)
Thank you. For not treating me like a file.

Cruz smiles, small and real.

CRUZ
You're a person. Go be one.

A buzz. The door opens. Light from the hallway, harsh and white. He walks into it.

Behind him, in the vent, one more tick. Nobody turns.

GIDEON
(to the empty room)
Damn pipes.
